{"author":{"id":"8d7a7fea5b07a0d5d081edd516d55afeb51ce9c18b2b2be6ceb30eb17cd9b547"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794304712,"nonce":"r6GAc2OcPrYZQQ1+aOD44sPr+so=","title":"Partial subtree rebuild (process_pending_subtree_rebuilds_routed) grows about quadratically with the number of sibling elements","message":"A live area whose content has N sibling elements (rows of a timeline, each with a few children) costs time that grows faster than N when it is rebuilt after a state change.\n\nMeasured (blinc_layout 0.6.0, release, offscreen, no window): one key press that changes a signal read by a stateful region, then one frame (take_pending_property_updates, apply_partial_property_updates, process_pending_subtree_rebuilds_routed, compute_layout). Region content: N absolutely positioned bars with a title and a progress child, in one scroll box.\n\nitems  seconds\n30     0.014\n60     0.032\n120    0.083\n240    0.252\n480    0.856\n\nDoubling the count multiplies the time by 2.3 to 3.4. A review of the same plan measured 874 ms at 480 items.\n\nThe cause is attributed to process_pending_subtree_rebuilds_routed by an independent review; I have not profiled inside Blinc, so which step is quadratic (diff of children, restyle, layout) is not established.\n\nReproducer: the ignored test series_of_item_counts in fs-components/crates/fs-components-timeline/tests/view_today.rs (cargo test --release -- --ignored --nocapture series).\n\nExpected: time for one rebuild roughly proportional to the number of changed or total nodes.\n\nStand: waits for the Blinc team / fork; no change made here.","files":null}]}